## Analyzing Logs with `journalctl`

Systemd includes a centralized logging facility (`systemd-journald`) that collects messages from the kernel, services, and stdout/stderr outputs.

```
[ Kernel / Services ] ➔ [ systemd-journald ] ➔ [ journalctl Query ] ➔ [ Filtered Output ]

```

### Log Inspection Commands

* `journalctl` displays all system logs starting from the oldest recorded entry.
* `journalctl -u service_name` filters log messages exclusively for a specified service (e.g., `journalctl -u sshd`).
* `journalctl -f` streams new log entries live as they are generated in real-time.
* `journalctl -b` displays only logs generated during the current system boot cycle.
* `journalctl -p err` filters logs to show only entries marked with error severity or higher.